File handling in C 
------------------

A named collection of data, stored in secondry storage is called as file. 

File is necessary because 
	
	* When a program is terminated, the entire data is lost. Storing in a file will preserve your data even if the program terminated. 
	* If we have to enter a large no.of data, it will take a lot of time to enter them all. If we have file containing all data, we can access the contents of the file using few command in c . 
	* Operations involved with files are 

		* Open the file 
		* Read/Write data operations on files
		* Close the file 

Library functions for files

	fopen()		fgetc()		fput()		fgets()		fputs()		fprintf()	fscanf()	fread()
	fwrite()	ftell()		fseek()		fclose()	rewind()	remove()

fopen()
-------

Prototype

	FILE *fopen (const char *path, const char *mode);

Description 
	
	fopen() function opens the file whose name in the string pointed by the path and associated of a stream with it. 

Return value : 

	Success : FILE * 	-> Non zero 
	Failure : NULL 		-> Zero 

	In a fopen() function first argument should be a path, if we provide direct file name it will search it in a present working directory. 

fgetc()
-------

Prototype 

	int fgetc(FILE *stream);

Description 
	
	fgetc() reads the next character from stream and returns it as an unsigned char cast to an int, or returns EOF on end of the file or error.

Return Value 

	Success : Unsigned character cast to integer 
	Failure : EOF or -1

If we create a file using vi editor, vi editor puts \n at the end of the file. This \n indicated no more character in a file. 


fputc()
-------

Prototype 

	int fputc(int c, FILE *stream);

Description 

	fputc() writes the character c, cast to an unsigned char, to stream.

Return Value 

	Success : character written as unsigned char cast to an int 
	Failure : EOF or -1
